=== entry 651cc2734134
kind: policy
rules: delegate, background, store, pr-wait
intent: Workers are async delegate_task children whose completions wake the coordinator; the program's tracker `notes.md` lives in the shared per-repo pstack state folder; PRs are watched with watch_pull_request. Policy (reload-the-orchestrator-after): reload the orchestrator after auto-compaction. The frontmatter PreToolUse hook records the coordinator's session id once; the project's settings.json SessionStart compact hook prints a reload directive and the state file for that session only. The Orchestrate playbook names the state file the coordinator keeps current, and references/compact-reload.md holds the settings snippet and script. Upstream dropped its store tables and drains, so the state file paragraph now follows this block and is rewritten with each `notes.md` update. It still applies while poteto-mode's frontmatter hook and compact-reload.md ship. Preserve: no scripts, no polling loops, no relaying between agents.
--- upstream
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `Task`. Keep the program's state in the Project tracker (`notes.md`) and the Project store. Watch each PR with PR and CI event subscriptions when your harness has them. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.
--- patched
Run it with your own tools. Spawn workers with `delegate_task` (`mode: "async"`), one call per worker with the whole kickoff in `task` and its own `clientRequestId`, and keep each `taskId` and `childThreadId`. A worker's completion wakes this thread, so end the turn instead of waiting on it. Keep the program's state in a tracker, `notes.md`, in the store (`~/.local/state/pstack/<repo>/`, where `<repo>` is `basename "$(dirname "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)")"`, created if missing). Watch each PR with `watch_pull_request`, which wakes this thread on a failed check, required checks passing, another person's comment or review, or a new conflict. Call no scripts, run no polling loops, and never relay messages or status between agents.

`$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/orchestrator-state.md` is the one file that survives compaction, so it lives in the project and not the store. Rewrite it each time you update `notes.md`, under 6,000 characters. It holds the standing orders, the live agents with their `taskId`s, the Pods, the open PRs, the decisions waiting on the user, and the next steps. The compact hook in `references/compact-reload.md` prints it back; install that hook in the project's `.claude/settings.json`, or the file is never read.

=== entry ca155dd357b3
kind: translation
rules: isolation, verify-surface, team-kit, transcripts, model-effort, resume, same-model-review
intent: A worker that writes gets its own worktree the way upstream put workers on a cloud VM, and a reader shares the checkout; the list of reasons to stay on this machine becomes a note that every child runs here. Effort is a ceiling from the role line. Upstream's fork has no T3 primitive, so (no rule fits) it becomes a new delegate_task pointed at the worker's worktree once that worker finishes; messaging a worker is t3_thread_send. Policy: the PR's verifier runs on its worker's model and effort unless the user names another (rule same-model-review). Preserve one owner per PR, the coordinator never editing code, the three-level cap and rolling window of about ten, and start with one unit.
--- upstream
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run workers on a new cloud VM (`environment: "cloud"`) unless the work needs this machine: a `control-ui` or `control-cli` runtime check (from `cursor-team-kit`), local transcripts, a simulator, or auth that exists only here. Pick each subagent's reasoning level and fast or standard speed for the job, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. Message a worker only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on a different model family. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
--- patched
- **One owner per PR.** The owner is the only writer of its branch and carries the PR from build to merge. Only the owner pushes, rebases, or retargets it. Closing or retargeting a base PR orphans every PR above it, so the owner does that only on your call. You never author or edit code. Conflicted merges, chain rebases, and fixes go to owners.
- **A fresh worker for each independent task.** Its kickoff carries everything it needs, including each upstream report it depends on, pasted in full. Workers can't see their siblings. Run each worker that writes in its own worktree (`isolation: "worktree"`), a checkout of your current local commit. Uncommitted edits are not copied, so commit first or name a pushed branch. A worker that only reads gets no `isolation` and shares your checkout, with scratch copies in `/tmp`. Every worker runs on this machine, so a runtime check (the project's own verification skill, `preview_*` tools for a UI, or the shell for a CLI), transcripts read with `t3_thread_read`, a simulator, and auth that exists only here work from any of them. Set each subagent's effort for the job, lowering its line's effort for routine work and never raising it, per the Subagents section.
- **A sub-coordinator only when one area has many parallel workers.** It owns that area's workers and kickoffs and sends you one rollup, never raw worker reports. Stop at three levels: you, sub-coordinators, and workers. Keep about ten workers in flight per coordinator, and start the next one as each finishes instead of waiting on a whole batch.
- **Fork when new work needs a worker's built state,** such as its checkout, uncommitted changes, or a running process. The fork is a new `delegate_task` whose kickoff gives that worker's worktree by absolute path and starts once that worker has finished, since a child starts with no conversation history. Message a worker (`t3_thread_send` to its `childThreadId`) only for a follow-up on the same work. Never message one to check on it, because a message restarts an idle agent.
- **Each PR gets a verifier that isn't its author,** on the same model and effort as the worker it checks, unless the user named another. The owner merges only on that verifier's pass at the current head.
- **Start with one unit.** Take it through kickoff, owner, verifier, and merge before you fan out, so a flaw in the kickoff costs one agent instead of fifty. On near-identical cheap units, the first unit is that test.
